WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
The first film showed two populations drawing from one limited supply.

2
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.440
If limits hold populations back, how can working together help?

3
00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.040
Gold food dots leave the tube and enter a honeybee colony.

4
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.200
The same resource now moves along green paths.

5
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.180
The picture changes, but the question about limited needs remains.

6
00:00:26.783 --> 00:00:32.023
Cooperation happens when members of a population work together to meet their needs.

7
00:00:33.363 --> 00:00:37.143
In a colony, workers collect food, guard the hive, and care for young.

8
00:00:38.423 --> 00:00:40.823
They also pass liquid food from bee to bee.

9
00:00:42.023 --> 00:00:43.663
The shares are not always equal.

10
00:00:44.843 --> 00:00:49.143
The picture separates the jobs so their different effects stay easy to follow.

11
00:00:50.233 --> 00:00:55.313
Imagine every worker collecting food while nobody guards or cares for young.

12
00:00:56.613 --> 00:00:57.913
Which needs would go unmet?

13
00:00:59.153 --> 00:01:03.073
Watch the green paths, and decide what different jobs add to the group.

14
00:01:04.233 --> 00:01:06.853
Hold the whole colony in mind, not one worker.

15
00:01:12.833 --> 00:01:15.533
Different jobs connect several needs at once.

16
00:01:16.753 --> 00:01:20.313
One worker brings food, another guards, and another tends young.

17
00:01:21.673 --> 00:01:24.793
Together, the workers meet several colony needs at once.

18
00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:29.813
Each linked job supports needs that one collector cannot cover.

19
00:01:31.553 --> 00:01:34.433
Cooperation helps, but every worker still needs resources.

20
00:01:35.717 --> 00:01:38.857
Other populations cooperate in different ways.

21
00:01:40.177 --> 00:01:42.517
A wolf pack can catch large prey such as elk.

22
00:01:43.577 --> 00:01:46.877
A tight school can make one fish harder for a predator to catch.

23
00:01:48.217 --> 00:01:51.257
Working together can help members get food or stay safe.

24
00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:54.930
Cooperation does not remove limits.

25
00:01:56.250 --> 00:02:00.910
A limiting factor is anything in the environment that can restrict a population.

26
00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:06.130
Living factors are biotic, such as food, disease, and predators.

27
00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:11.230
Nonliving factors are abiotic, such as water and temperature.

28
00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:16.390
Shelter can be living or nonliving, depending on what provides it.

29
00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:20.270
Populations in a community are interdependent.

30
00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:23.450
A change in one can affect another.

31
00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:28.850
If disease reduces a rabbit population, foxes have less food.

32
00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:34.550
The disease strikes rabbits directly, then reaches foxes through their food link.

33
00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:39.770
The foxes can be affected even if the disease never infects them.

34
00:02:40.783 --> 00:02:46.403
Limited resources can restrict populations; cooperation can help members meet their

35
00:02:46.403 --> 00:02:50.023
needs. Both can happen in the same population.

36
00:02:51.543 --> 00:02:54.703
Cooperation changes what members can accomplish together.

37
00:02:56.023 --> 00:02:58.143
Help and limits belong in the same picture.

38
00:02:59.303 --> 00:03:03.623
It does not make food, water, shelter, disease, or predators disappear.
